
  - [1mDue dates / time-based reminders[0m — tasks get a [38;5;153mdueAt[39m; a scheduler fires when one comes due or goes overdue. This is
    the only genuinely [3mnon-self[0m change source available without a backend: time moves whether you act or not. Costs you
    a date field and a timer loop. Makes the notification list meaningful even on first load.
  - [1mOwn-action feedback + undo[0m — toast on create/complete/delete, with undo. Genuinely useful, cheap, but it's a
    feedback system; nothing accumulates, nothing is "unread." Rules out a notification centre having anything in it.
  - [1mSimulated external actor[0m — a fake collaborator mutates tasks on a timer so the prototype [3mdemos[0m like the real
    product. Good for showing stakeholders, dishonest as a foundation — you'd be building the consumer of an event
    stream you'll later throw away.

  Cheap to change later in all three: adding channels (toast vs. bell-icon centre vs. browser [38;5;153mNotification[39m API) — that's
  a delivery seam. Expensive to change later: the event model. If notifications only ever describe your own clicks,
  there's no subscription concept, and adding one later means reworking how tasks and notifications relate.

  [1mMy recommendation: due dates, with the event source behind a small interface.[0m It's the one option where the feature is
  real rather than simulated, it gives "tasks they care about" an actual meaning (you care about what's coming due),
  and the interface means a future backend event stream plugs into the same consumer. I'd add own-action toasts as a
  second source later if you want them — same bus, no redesign.
